00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.560
Ok, so here is the news. We have this&nbsp;
text-to-image generation model DALL-E 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.880
from OpenAI. It’s a diffusion model that can&nbsp;
take in text and produce an amazing picture.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.400
If you are interested in how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.400
text-to-image diffusion models work,&nbsp;
check out our previous videos on this!

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.040
But now, here’s the catch: DALL-E 2 can not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.720
take in sensible input text and produce&nbsp;
its best shots at an image from this,&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.840
but no. It can take in gibberish and&nbsp;
produce output that looks really good.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.800
And even more, it seems like this “gibberish”&nbsp;
does mean something for the model because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.920
combination of something that produces&nbsp;
birds with something that produces bugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:22.160
in "Apoploe vesrreaitais eating&nbsp;
Contarra ccetnxniams luryca tanniounons"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.960
gives images of birds eating&nbsp;
bugs! Is this language?

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.800
No, it is not, as the authors have quickly&nbsp;
realized after announcing their findings on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.880
Twitter and changed their paper title from&nbsp;
“secret language” to “secret vocabulary”.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.200
Language has syntax and grammar, but DALL-E&nbsp;
2 seems rather to respond at gibberish words.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.680
So how did the authors find and generate&nbsp;
such examples? It seems like the discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.880
happened while the authors were playing with&nbsp;
the text rendering capabilities of DALL-E 2.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.680
The model is already known to&nbsp;
produce very weird results on this.&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.160
So, they gave the model an input&nbsp;
where it had to produce text, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:12.080
"Two farmers talking about vegetables, with&nbsp;
subtitles" and found that the produced text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:18.720
is not as random as one might think.
 
This word “Vicootess” produces dishes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.480
with vegetables, and “apoploe&nbsp;
vesrreaitars” produces birds.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.800
So the authors concluded that the farmers must be&nbsp;
talking about birds messing with their vegetables.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.320
In this thread, you can see other examples too,&nbsp;
but what we see there and in the paper are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.840
really many examples and it is a bit unclear&nbsp;
on what the success rate of this method is.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.800
Sometimes it works, sometimes it&nbsp;
doesn’t as highlighted in this thread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.880
where only 4 out of 10 examples generate&nbsp;
sports from the word “tiboer”, even though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:59.040
like in the farmers example, those two men speak&nbsp;
about “tiboer” which seems to be about sports.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.240
So why does this even happen that DALL-E&nbsp;
2 generates good looking pictures from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.360
gibberish? The authors make the fuzzy claim&nbsp;
that the model has internally developed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.760
hidden “language”, ahm, we mean “vocabulary”&nbsp;
after being trained on image-text pairs!

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.280
Well, this is not very specific,&nbsp;
so let’s look at the theories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.160
circulating on Twitter right now, because&nbsp;
there are a few very interesting ones:

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.200
The first one I saw was by Joscha Bach.&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.120
He points out that the text generations&nbsp;
are quite inconsistent. In the paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:39.280
the authors prompt DALL-E 2 with “"Two&nbsp;
whales talking about food, with subtitles",&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.280
but Joscha Bach points out that the “secret words”&nbsp;
one gets from this, are different every time.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.560
So, Joscha Bach goes on saying that the&nbsp;
secret words are just random strings that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.520
sometimes point to consistent regions the&nbsp;
embedding space. The model projects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.520
samples into a high dimensional space&nbsp;
and clusters neighboring concepts.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.920
Then honestly, I do not get what the&nbsp;
rest of the explanation wants to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.280
maybe you know, so go to the&nbsp;
comments and explain if you want.:)

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.760
So here is Ms. Coffee Bean’s take on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.920
which might be very different from&nbsp;
the one in this thread, sorry.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.720
The model has a limited number of&nbsp;
parameters and for efficiency reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.160
it needs to cluster the space of words well,&nbsp;
so concepts that are related to one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:28.640
should be close in this space. Since&nbsp;
we are working with neural networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:34.320
the text it represented as continuous&nbsp;
vectors, but surprise! Text is discrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.760
and is composed of words that do not have&nbsp;
an in-between as a continuous vectors do.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:42.080
And since the model always must produce an output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:47.840
whatever the input, the in-between words,&nbsp;
so gibberish will also produce an output.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.200
As it turns out, this is sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.360
consistent between the string input&nbsp;
and rendered text in the image.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.960
How seldom this works and how inconsistent this&nbsp;
is, is highlighted by another twitter user, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:06.000
points out that the bug producing prompt does&nbsp;
not produce so many bugs or pests after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.200
since we in DALL-E 2’s generations, we&nbsp;
also have animals like deer and goats.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:14.560
Still along the line of the&nbsp;
discreteness of language are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.960
the opinions that say that the “secret&nbsp;
language” is mostly due to tokenizers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:26.560
effects since “Apo-didae &amp; Plo-ceidae are&nbsp;
families of birds, each with 100+ species.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:32.160
Apo-diformes is the biggest order of&nbsp;
birds with 400+ species of birds.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.600
So the model has connected the&nbsp;
“apo” and “plo” subwords to birds.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.720
Somebody else found that one can infer some of&nbsp;
these sub-words that will work with DALL-E 2 by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:49.920
looking at Wikipedia, where “Actinopterygii"&nbsp;
and "Placodermi" are two families of fish.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.040
And then "placoactin knunfidg"&nbsp;
generates fish images.&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:59.680
You know, with neural networks,&nbsp;
sometimes it is great input in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:05.280
garbage out. Now it is gibberish&nbsp;
in, quality images out!

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.720
It’s clear that we do not yet understand these&nbsp;
black boxes and unless some more rigorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.520
interpretability goes into these models,&nbsp;
we will continue prompting in the dark.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:22.000
It’s important to highlight that as long&nbsp;
as we are surprised by this weird way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.760
communication from DALL-E 2 and similar models,&nbsp;
we are also ignorant of some security issues:

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:33.120
Adversarial attacks with these gibberish&nbsp;
prompts, as well as not-so-gibberish prompts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:39.680
can trigger unwanted behavior from models,&nbsp;
circumventing filters on the allowed inputs.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.720
It’s clear that so far, these models do not&nbsp;
behave like humans would expect them to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.280
and malicious humans could take advantage of this.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.960
What do you think about this? Would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.840
interested to enroll in Ms. Coffee&nbsp;
Bean’s DALL-E 2 language classes?

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:10.607
See you next time! Okay, bye!

